{"kind": "report", "step": 11, "ts": "2026-11-19T16:24:34.545321+00:00", "data": {"category": "wan", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "wan_nic_driver", "cause": "The firewall's WAN interface vtnet0 has wedged: its virtio transmit queue stopped draining (repeated 'watchdog timeout on queue 0'), so nothing can be sent out to the ISP even though the link shows active and the lease is present.", "evidence": ["dmesg -a: repeated 'vtnet0: watchdog timeout on queue 0' with alternating 'link state changed to DOWN/UP' cycles \u2014 classic stuck virtio queue.", "ping -c 3 198.51.100.1 on gw.lan: 'ping: sendto: No buffer space available', 100% loss to the directly-connected next hop, while netstat -m shows no mbuf exhaustion (0 denied) \u2014 TX path blocked at the device, not memory.", "arp -n: next hop 198.51.100.1 '(incomplete)' \u2014 ARP unresolved because probes cannot egress the hung NIC.", "netstat -i: vtnet0 Oerrs=48211 with Ipkts still counted, consistent with a failing transmit side.", "ifconfig -a: vtnet0 status active with valid inet 198.51.100.27; netstat -rn has a valid default via 198.51.100.1 \u2014 addressing/routing are intact, so this is not a lease or routing fault.", "/var/db/wan-watchdog/state.json + events.jsonl: outage_start ~02:06, four WAN reloads during the outage all rc=0 yet connectivity never returned, and reloads_24h hit the cap of 12 \u2014 DHCP reconfiguration does not clear the hang.", "modem-events.jsonl / modem-metrics.jsonl: a 'No Ranging Response - T3 time-out' at 02:01:44 preceded the outage and the modem rebooted/re-registered at 02:28:21; it now reports OPERATIONAL (uptime 5280s), so the provider path recovered but the firewall's WAN side did not."], "action": "reboot_host", "recommendation": "The WAN NIC driver on gw.lan is wedged (virtio queue watchdog timeouts, sendto ENOBUFS, unresolved next-hop ARP). Renewing the WAN lease will not help \u2014 the watchdog already reloaded the interface repeatedly with no recovery. Reboot the firewall to reset the vtnet0 device/driver; that is the least disruptive action that clears a hung NIC queue. Separately, note the ISP-side cable-modem T3 timeout/reboot at 02:01 as the trigger for the original drop, but the current outage is held by the local NIC hang.", "detail": "WAN NIC vtnet0 transmit queue hung (watchdog timeouts)", "fix": "firewall_reboot", "contributing": {"host": "gw.lan", "component": "modem"}}}
